y about a young assistant Laura  Madden being asked to sort of exchange  massages with Harvey Weinstein in a  hotel in Dublin in 1991 there's another  set of allegations in our story about an  assistant being forced to give him a  naked massage in a hotel room in Los  Angeles in 2015 two different places in  the world two different women  know each other whatsoever and yet the  pattern is so similar there are also  lots of settlements yes so there is a  legal and financial trail to these  allegations I think it's important to  explain what a settlement is often when  a woman feels that she's been sexually  harassed the way it's resolved if the  settlement is reached the women the  woman gets some financial recompense  however the thing that is a little more  concerning is that the women often have  to sign a confidentiality agreement  seeing that they won't tell anybody  would happened so the big question of  our story is how could Harvey Weinstein  enjoy all of this public acclaim collect  all these Oscars over the years while in  the background in private these  allegations are piling up so settlements  are part of the answer because the women  who have these stories in many cases  were not allowed to speak publicly about  them how did all this come about Jody  because you're saying that you even  started investigating Harvey Weinstein  and you're saying a lot of people knew  about this that is the question  we spoke to dozens of former Miramax and  weinstein company employees who said  that they knew that something was wrong  in the workplace either they were  victims alleged victims themselves some  of them knew stories from colleagues or  had witnessed things and then others  said that they just had this vague sort  of you know half knowledge about  something not me and quite right the  number of them who said that they did  anything is very small so I think the  question is who protected the women and  who protected Harvey Weinstein  well Jody why now actually judge said  this happened 20 years ago what has led  to this particular moment well I think  in part it's about the commitment that  the times has to telling these stories  about sexual harassment we've done  reporting in the last year we broke the  Bill O'Reilly story about allegations of  sexual harassment at Fox we've reported  on very similar allegations in Silicon  Valley where female entrepreneurs felt  they were pressured by investors and as  investigative reporters we feel like we  have this commitment to putting the  puzzle pieces together and asking the  big questions about these stories what  about the attitude of men within the  company and the board of directors it's  a great question both men and  women helped us with this reporting and  there's a really striking quote from a  guy named Marc Gill a former Miramax  executive in the story he says that at  Miramax everything looked golden on the  outside but inside it was a mess and the  treatment of women was the biggest mess  of all wine scenes attorney tells us  this that your investigation relies on  mostly hearsay accounts and a faulty  report we sent the times the facts and  evidence but they ignored it and rushed  to publish now the lawyer did not  respond to our request to elaborate on  what he sent you all but what what is  your response that that they sent you  evidence that refutes this Harvey  Weinstein in fact has called the paper  reckless what do you say to that well  first of all the story relies on a lot  of documentation on the record names of  women dates of incidents and remember  that one of our sources was internal  company records we were able to find out  that some of these allegations were  documented inside the companies and yet  never shared with the public and  arguably never addressed that well as  far as our interactions with them they  knew our story was coming for months we  shared the allegations in the story with  them before publication and they had a  chance to address them and we stand by  the accuracy and the fairness of our  reporting have other women come forward  since this story will see Jodi Kanter  thank you very much for joining us at  the table today
 

